          "case_name": "Roe v. Texas Department of Protective & Regulatory Services",
          "citation": "299 F.3d 395 (5th Cir. 2002)",
          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit",
          "year": 2002,
          "date_decided": "2002-07-17",
          "holding": "The Fifth Circuit held that the plaintiffs raised a fact question whether the caseworker violated the child's Fourth Amendment rights, but reversed on qualified immunity because those rights were not clearly established, while holding the mother had consented to the investigative home visit and that silence cannot supply consent.",
          "relevance": "Establishes that a family's cooperation with voluntary, non-court services must rest on affirmative consent, not acquiescence.",
          "quote": "Although they still must obtain the parent's verbal, affirmative consent before conducting an investigative home interview, they need not obtain that consent again when they appear at the parent's doorstep.",
